  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nLeftover convective debris clouds over SW New Mexico have mostly\ndissipated, except around an MCV in the Gila Region. Convection\nhas been mostly confined to the Sacramento Mountains, though we\nare seeing some buildups in the Bootheel and even over a few \ndesert mountain ranges. \n\nStorms over the Sacramentos have had a small footprint, but have\nbeen training and anchored to the terrain a bit. But intensity is\non the downturn. Overall, expect fairly isolated thunderstorms\ngoing forward, but still favoring areas east of the Rio Grande,\nand mostly southern Otero and near higher terrain in Hudspeth\nCounty.\n\nModels continue to show increasing moisture for tomorrow (Wed),\nbut low level moisture will be lacking in northern areas, with \nPWAT values by afternoon ranging from around 1.00 inches in\ncentral Sierra County, to 1.25 inches near the International\nborder, where modest instability will also be present. Upper \nlevel support will be limited, as the upper ridge will be trying\nto rebuild its western limb. But, isolated thunderstorms will\nstill be possible, favoring southern areas, and especially the\nBootheel.\n\nThursday, PWAT values continue to increase and advect northwards.\nTroughing over the western US looks to try and edge out the\nwestern limb of the ridge again. No strong shortwave troughs are\nnoted, but some pockets of cooler mid-level air are evident in the\nGFS. Also, upper level diffluence returns as the ridge high aloft\nbreaks down. Overall, Thursday looks like an uptick in coverage,\nwith southwestern New Mexico most favored in the evening. \n\nThe subtropical ridge continues to hold all the cards for precip\nchances going forward. SW New Mexico and Arizona look more favored\nover the weekend as the ridge rebuilds over the area yet again.\nBut, there appears to be plenty of trapped moisture underneath.\nBut 500mb temps of -3.5 to -4C are not favorable for areas east of\nthe Divide. \n\nTemperatures still look to hold below 100 through the weekend,\nthough early next week is trending towards a more sprawling ridge\nagain.",
